              "text": "Depending on the spelling and context of your question, you are likely referring to one of two prominent figures:\n\n### 1. Katherine McCoy (Graphic Designer & Educator)\nIf you are referring to the spelling **Katherine McCoy** (born October 12, 1945), she is a highly influential American graphic designer and educator. \n* **Cranbrook Academy of Art:** She is best known for co-chairing the graduate design program at the Cranbrook Academy of Art alongside her husband, Michael McCoy, from 1971 to 1995. During her tenure, she helped reshape graphic design education by pushing boundaries beyond traditional High Modernism, incorporating post-structuralism, critical theory, and pluralism into design thinking.\n* **Academic Career:** In addition to Cranbrook, she has served as a Senior Lecturer at the Illinois Institute of Technology\u2019s Institute of Design and as a Distinguished Visiting Professor at the Royal College of Art in London.\n* **Awards:** In 1999, she was awarded the prestigious AIGA Medal (the highest honor in the American design profession) for her massive impact on design practice and education. \n\n### 2. Dr. Kathy / Kathleen McCoy (Microbiome Researcher)\nIf you are looking for a figure in the field of science, **Dr. Kathleen McCoy** is a highly regarded immunologist. \n* **Current Role:** She is a Professor in the Department of Physiology and Pharmacology at the University of Calgary's Cumming School of Medicine.\n* **Research:** She is the Scientific Director of the International Microbiome Center and holds the Killam Memorial Chair at the University of Calgary. Her research focuses on the relationship between the gut microbiome and the immune system, particularly how exposure to microbes early in life can prevent immunological diseases like allergies, asthma, and inflammatory bowel disease. \n\n***\n\n*Note: If you are looking for someone else (such as **Catherine McCoy**, the Australian naturopath and founder of iNaturally, or a local professional), please feel free to provide more context!*",
